TMB — Triangle Map Binary Format
Version: 1.2
Format ID: 54 4D 42 03
Status: Draft
Keywords: MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, MAY are as defined in RFC 2119.


================================================================
1. PURPOSE, SCOPE, AND NON-GOALS
================================================================

TMB is a compact binary serialization of indexed triangle meshes
extracted from a glTF 2.0 asset, intended for consumption by
renderers and shape-analysis tools that require:

  - deterministic layout and deterministic bytes;
  - fast sequential parsing;
  - file size competitive with, or smaller than, the source glTF;
  - explicit, testable conformance rules.

1.1 Scope

  TMB is defined only for triangle primitives (glTF mode TRIANGLES,
  or absent mode, which per the glTF specification defaults to
  TRIANGLES). A conforming writer MUST reject any input primitive
  whose declared mode is not TRIANGLES.

  A conforming writer MUST reject input primitives whose geometry
  is delivered through a compressed intermediate (Draco, meshopt).
  Compressed geometry MUST be decompressed before conversion.

  TMB stores vertex positions in world space (section 3.3). TMB
  does NOT preserve the source node hierarchy, local vertex
  coordinates, animations, skins, morph targets, cameras, or
  textures. TMB is not an interchange format for glTF and MUST NOT
  be used where round-trip reconstruction of the source document is
  required.

1.2 Non-goals

  TMB does not define a material model beyond a name and a numeric
  slot index. TMB does not carry image data. TMB does not define a
  scene graph. TMB does not support streaming; readers may assume
  the entire file is available before parsing. TMB does not define
  cross-descriptor vertex or index sharing; instanced primitives
  appear as independent descriptors with duplicated geometry
  (section 10.3).

1.3 Endianness

  TMB is little-endian on all platforms. Conforming writers on
  big-endian platforms MUST serialize explicitly little-endian.
  Conforming readers MAY assume all multi-byte integers and all
  floating-point values are little-endian.


================================================================
2. CONFORMANCE AND NOTATION
================================================================

A conforming reader accepts every file valid under section 7 and
rejects every file invalid under section 7.

A conforming writer produces files valid under section 7.

"Byte offset" is a zero-based count of bytes from a stated origin.
"Region" is a contiguous run of bytes identified by name and
offset. "Blob" is a region containing homogeneous typed data.

Sizes are denoted:

  Nv[k]   vertex count of mesh entry k
  Nt[k]   triangle count of mesh entry k
  Ni[k]   index count of mesh entry k, equal to 3 * Nt[k]
  W[k]    index width in bytes of mesh entry k (1, 2, or 4)
  Av[k]   attribute region byte length of mesh entry k
  H       byte length of the header region
  V       byte length of the vertex blob
  I       byte length of the index blob
  A       byte length of the attribute blob
  K       number of mesh entries (descriptors in the header)

This specification defines TMB version 1.2, which uses format ID
0x54 0x4D 0x42 0x03. Prior versions 1.0 (0x01) and 1.1 (0x02) are
rejected by version 1.2 readers without attempt at best-effort
parsing.


================================================================
3. TERMINOLOGY
================================================================

3.1 Tri-map

  A tri-map is an ordered collection of K mesh entries. Each entry
  comprises a descriptor (metadata), a vertex array (Nv float32
  triples), an index array (Ni integers of width W), and an
  optional attribute region.

3.2 Triangle

  A triangle in a mesh entry is the ordered triple of consecutive
  index values at positions 3*m, 3*m+1, 3*m+2 in the index array,
  for m in [0, Nt). Winding order is preserved as authored.

3.3 Local and world vertex space

  Local vertex space: coordinates before any per-node transform.

  World vertex space: coordinates after multiplying each local
  position by the mesh entry's world_matrix as a column vector:
  v_world = M · v_local.

  TMB stores vertices in WORLD vertex space. A conforming reader
  MUST treat the stored vertex coordinates as final and MUST NOT
  reapply world_matrix.

  TMB stores normals, when present, in WORLD vertex space, already
  transformed per section 8.12, sign-corrected per section 8.13,
  and renormalized.

3.4 Material slot

  The integer index into the source glTF's "materials" array. -1
  denotes no material assigned.


================================================================
4. BYTE LAYOUT
================================================================

A TMB file consists of five consecutive regions:

  Offset          Length   Region
  --------------  -------  ----------------------------------
  0               4        Magic
  4               4        Header length (uint32 LE)
  8               H        Header (H = header length)
  8 + H           V        Vertex blob
  8 + H + V       I        Index blob
  8 + H + V + I   A        Attribute blob

  Total file length = 8 + H + V + I + A.

All multi-byte integers are little-endian. All floating-point
values are IEEE 754 binary32, little-endian. TMB does not use
IEEE 754 binary64 anywhere.

A conforming writer MUST produce a file whose length equals
8 + H + V + I + A. A conforming reader MUST reject a file whose
length does not equal that value. The minimum valid file length is
10 bytes (K = 0, header "[]", all blobs empty).


================================================================
5. REGION DEFINITIONS
================================================================

5.1 Magic (4 bytes, offset 0)

  The four bytes 0x54, 0x4D, 0x42, 0x03, in that order. This
  sequence identifies a TMB version 1.2 file. Any other sequence,
  including 0x54 0x4D 0x42 0x01 and 0x54 0x4D 0x42 0x02, MUST be
  rejected.

5.2 Header length (4 bytes, offset 4)

  Unsigned 32-bit little-endian integer equal to H.

  Conforming writers MUST emit H such that 8 + H is less than or
  equal to the total file length.

  Conforming readers MUST reject H such that 8 + H exceeds the
  total file length.

5.3 Header (H bytes, offset 8)

  A JSON array of K mesh descriptor objects (K >= 0), UTF-8
  encoded with no byte order mark, no trailing newline, and no
  whitespace outside of JSON string values. A conforming reader
  MUST reject a Header region that is not valid UTF-8, not valid
  JSON, or not a JSON array.

  Field definitions are in section 6.

5.4 Vertex blob (V bytes, offset 8 + H)

  Concatenation, in header-array order, of each mesh entry's
  vertex array:

    V = sum over k of (Nv[k] * 12)

  Each mesh entry contributes Nv[k] consecutive float32 triples
  (x, y, z) in world vertex space.

  The byte position of mesh entry k is given by vertex_offset[k]
  (section 6.11).

5.5 Index blob (I bytes, offset 8 + H + V)

  Concatenation, in header-array order, of each mesh entry's
  index array:

    I = sum over k of (Nt[k] * 3 * W[k])

  Each mesh entry contributes Nt[k] * 3 consecutive unsigned
  integers of width W[k] bytes each. Values are ABSOLUTE offsets
  into the mesh entry's own vertex array; they are NOT
  delta-encoded. Conforming writers MUST NOT store delta-encoded
  indices.

  The byte position of mesh entry k is given by index_offset[k]
  (section 6.12).

5.6 Attribute blob (A bytes, offset 8 + H + V + I)

  Concatenation, in header-array order, of each mesh entry's
  attribute region:

    A = sum over k of Av[k]

  For mesh entry k, the region is laid out as follows:

    - one 4-byte-aligned block per attribute declared in
      attr_layout[k], in the order declared in attr_layout[k];
    - each block contains Nv[k] elements of the declared type;
    - each element contains "components" values of "type";
    - block byte length = Nv[k] * components * sizeof(type);
    - if the current write position within the region is not a
      multiple of 4, pad with zero bytes to the next multiple of 4
      before writing the block.

  A conforming reader MUST treat padding bytes as zero-valued and
  MUST NOT interpret them as attribute data. A conforming writer
  MUST emit zero-valued padding.

  The byte position of mesh entry k's attribute region is
  attr_offset[k] (section 6.14).


================================================================
6. HEADER DESCRIPTOR FIELDS
================================================================

The Header region is a JSON array. Each element is a JSON object
(a "descriptor"). Every descriptor MUST contain every field listed
below. Fields typed "integer" MUST be serialized as JSON numbers
with no fractional part; a conforming reader MUST accept integer
values in the range [-2^31, 2^31 - 1] and MAY reject values with a
fractional part or outside that range. Fields typed "number"
additionally accept fractional and exponent forms.

A descriptor that omits a required or nullable field, or that
supplies a field with a JSON type inconsistent with its definition,
MUST be rejected.

6.1 source (string, required)

  Scene-graph node name from which the mesh was extracted. May be
  empty. Identification only.

6.2 mesh_index (integer >= 0, required)

  Zero-based index of the source mesh in the input glTF document's
  "meshes" array.

6.3 primitive_index (integer >= 0, required)

  Zero-based index of the primitive within that source mesh's
  "primitives" array.

6.4 material_index (integer >= -1, required)

  Material slot referenced by the source primitive, or -1 if
  absent.

6.5 material_name (string, nullable)

  If material_index == -1, MUST be JSON null.
  If material_index >= 0, MUST be the value of "name" on the
  referenced material, or JSON null if the material has no name.

  The string value "none" is not reserved and is a legal material
  name; the presence of a material is indicated only by
  material_index != -1.

6.6 world_matrix (array of 4 arrays of 4 numbers, required)

  A 4x4 affine transform, ROW-MAJOR: world_matrix[i][j] is the
  element in row i, column j. Column 3 of rows 0..2 holds the
  translation. Vertices transform as column vectors:
  v_world = M · v_local.

  The bottom row (world_matrix[3][0..3]) MUST be exactly equal to
  [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0] when each element is interpreted as an IEEE
  754 double and compared using the == operator. In particular,
  the value -0.0 (negative zero) is equal to 0.0 under ==, and
  both are accepted.

  The 3x3 block M[:3,:3] MUST be invertible. A conforming writer
  MUST reject any input whose transform produces a singular 3x3
  block. A conforming reader MUST reject a descriptor whose 3x3
  block is singular.

  world_matrix is informational only; see section 3.3.

6.7 n_verts (integer in [1, 2^31 - 1], required)

  Number of vertices in this mesh entry.

6.8 n_tris (integer in [1, 2^30 - 1], required)

  Number of triangles in this mesh entry. The upper bound
  2^30 - 1 ensures n_tris * 3 and n_tris * 12 both fit in
  signed 32-bit range.

6.9 n_indices (integer >= 3, required)

  Number of entries in the index array. MUST equal 3 * n_tris.

6.10 index_offset (integer in [0, 2^31 - 1], required)

  Byte offset of this mesh entry's index array within the index
  blob. For the first descriptor this MUST be 0. For each
  subsequent descriptor k > 0, this MUST equal
  index_offset[k-1] + Nt[k-1] * 3 * W[k-1].

6.11 vertex_offset (integer in [0, 2^31 - 1], required)

  Byte offset of this mesh entry's vertex array within the vertex
  blob. For the first descriptor this MUST be 0. For each
  subsequent descriptor k > 0, this MUST equal
  vertex_offset[k-1] + Nv[k-1] * 12.

6.12 index_width (integer in {1, 2, 4}, required)

  Byte width of each index.

  A conforming writer MUST select the smallest W in {1, 2, 4} such
  that n_verts <= 2^(8 * W). That is:

    if n_verts <= 256:      index_width = 1
    elif n_verts <= 65536:  index_width = 2
    else:                   index_width = 4

  A conforming reader MUST accept any W in {1, 2, 4} that
  satisfies n_verts <= 2^(8 * W) and MUST reject W not in
  {1, 2, 4} or too small to address index n_verts - 1.

6.13 attr_offset (integer in [0, 2^31 - 1], required)

  Byte offset of this mesh entry's attribute region within the
  attribute blob. For the first descriptor this MUST be 0. For
  each subsequent descriptor k > 0, this MUST equal
  attr_offset[k-1] + Av[k-1].

6.14 attr_length (integer >= 0, required)

  Byte length of this mesh entry's attribute region within the
  attribute blob. MUST equal the sum over attr_layout of:

    padded_align(Nv * components * sizeof(type), 4)

  where padded_align(x, 4) = ceil(x / 4) * 4, and sizeof(type) is
  4 for f32, 2 for u16, 2 for i16. Padding bytes MUST be zero. A
  mesh entry with an empty attr_layout MUST set attr_length to 0.

6.15 attr_layout (array of objects, required)

  Ordered sequence of attribute declarations. Array elements MUST
  be sorted by the "name" field in strict ascending byte order of
  their UTF-8 encodings (this is required for determinism; see
  §6.17). Each element MUST contain:

    name        (string)  one of "normals", "uvs", or an extension
                          name of the form "ext:<vendor>:<attr>"
                          where <vendor> and <attr> each match
                          [a-z0-9_]+
    type        (string)  one of "f32", "u16", "i16"
    components  (integer) positive integer

  For name == "uvs", components MUST be 2 and type MUST be one of
  {f32, u16, i16}.
  For name == "normals", components MUST be 3 and type MUST be
  "f32".
  For extension names, type and components are producer-defined.

  attr_layout MUST NOT contain two entries with the same name.

6.16 Absence of uvs and normals

  There are no separate "uvs_present" or "normals_present"
  boolean fields. Presence of an attribute is determined solely by
  the presence of an entry in attr_layout with the corresponding
  name. This eliminates the possibility of the boolean and the
  layout disagreeing.

  A reader that needs to know whether a mesh has UVs MUST scan
  attr_layout for an entry named "uvs". The scan is O(K_attr) with
  K_attr typically 0, 1, or 2.

  When normals are present, they MUST be unit-length in world
  vertex space to within the tolerance in §7.25.

6.17 Canonical JSON

  Conforming writers MUST emit the header JSON in canonical form
  so that two conforming writers producing the same in-memory
  representation produce byte-identical headers:

    6.17.1  No whitespace outside of string values. No trailing
            newline. No byte order mark.

    6.17.2  Object keys appear in strict ascending order of their
            UTF-8 byte encodings.

    6.17.3  Integers are emitted without a decimal point, without
            an exponent, and without leading zeros. The only
            integer that may begin with '0' is 0 itself.

    6.17.4  Floats are emitted with the shortest decimal string
            that round-trips to the same binary32 value under
            ECMAScript Number.prototype.toString() semantics
            (equivalently, the algorithm specified in RFC 8785
            §3.2.2.3). This is deterministic across conforming
            implementations: one specific byte sequence per
            float value.

    6.17.5  Negative zero is emitted as "0" not "-0".

    6.17.6  Strings are UTF-8 encoded. ASCII control characters
            (U+0000 through U+001F), '"', and '\' are escaped as
            specified in RFC 8259 §7. Non-ASCII characters are
            emitted unescaped.

    6.17.7  Null is emitted as the four bytes n, u, l, l.

    6.17.8  Booleans are emitted as true or false.

  A conforming reader MUST accept non-canonical header JSON so
  long as it is valid JSON. Canonical form is a writer
  requirement, not a reader requirement.

  For the world_matrix array and other float-valued fields, the
  canonical form is §6.17.4. This pins the byte sequence a reader
  will see for a given matrix, but does not imply that two
  writers given the same source glTF will necessarily produce
  byte-identical output if the source matrix was computed
  differently (for example, different matrix-decomposition order
  in the exporter).


================================================================
7. VALIDATION
================================================================

A conforming reader MUST reject a TMB file if any of the
following conditions hold:

7.1   The first four bytes are not 0x54 0x4D 0x42 0x03.
7.2   The file length is less than 10.
7.3   The value of H causes 8 + H to exceed the file length.
7.4   The Header region is not valid UTF-8.
7.5   The Header region is not valid JSON.
7.6   The Header region does not decode to a JSON array.
7.7   Any descriptor is not a JSON object, omits a required or
      nullable field, or supplies a field with a JSON type
      inconsistent with section 6.
7.8   Any descriptor has n_verts < 1, n_verts > 2^31 - 1,
      n_tris < 1, or n_tris > 2^30 - 1.
7.9   Any descriptor has n_indices != 3 * n_tris.
7.10  Any descriptor has index_width not in {1, 2, 4}, or has
      index_width too small to satisfy n_verts <= 2^(8 * W).
7.11  Any descriptor's world_matrix bottom row is not equal to
      [0, 0, 0, 1] under IEEE 754 double == comparison
      (section 6.6).
7.12  Any descriptor's world_matrix 3x3 block is singular.
7.13  For each k, vertex_offset[k] != sum_{j<k}(Nv[j] * 12).
7.14  For each k, index_offset[k] != sum_{j<k}(Nt[j] * 3 * W[j]).
7.15  For each k, attr_offset[k] != sum_{j<k}(Av[j]).
7.16  Any descriptor's attr_length does not equal the sum over
      attr_layout of padded_align(Nv * components * sizeof(type),
      4).
7.17  Any descriptor's attr_layout violates §6.15: unsorted
      names, duplicate names, illegal extension name characters,
      or inconsistent type/components for known names.
7.18  V != sum over k of (Nv[k] * 12).
7.19  I != sum over k of (Nt[k] * 3 * W[k]).
7.20  A != sum over k of Av[k].
7.21  The total file length is not exactly 8 + H + V + I + A.
7.22  Any index value in any mesh entry is greater than or equal
      to that mesh entry's n_verts.
7.23  Any float32 value in the vertex blob is a signaling NaN.
7.24  Any float32 value in the vertex blob is an infinity or a
      quiet NaN.
7.25  When attr_layout declares "normals", any stored normal
      triple (nx, ny, nz) does not satisfy:
      |sqrt(nx^2 + ny^2 + nz^2) - 1| <= 1e-4.
7.26  Any float32 value in the attribute blob (excluding padding)
      is a signaling NaN.
7.27  When material_index == -1, material_name is not JSON null.
7.28  When material_index >= 0, material_name is not JSON null
      or a string.

A conforming reader SHOULD reject a TMB file if:

7.29  Any float32 value in the attribute blob is an infinity or a
      quiet NaN.
7.30  The header region length H exceeds an implementation-
      defined maximum.
7.31  The number of descriptors K exceeds an implementation-
      defined maximum.
7.32  The total file length exceeds an implementation-defined
      maximum.

A conforming reader MUST NOT reject a TMB file on the sole basis
of any condition not listed in 7.1 through 7.28.


================================================================
8. WRITER REQUIREMENTS
================================================================

A conforming writer MUST:

8.1   Emit exactly the byte sequence 0x54 0x4D 0x42 0x03 in the
      first four bytes.
8.2   Emit H as a little-endian uint32.
8.3   Emit the Header region as UTF-8 encoded canonical compact
      JSON per section 6.17.
8.4   Include every required and nullable field (section 6) with
      the declared JSON type in every descriptor.
8.5   Emit the vertex blob of length V = sum(Nv * 12) at byte
      offset 8 + H.
8.6   Emit the index blob of length I = sum(Nt * 3 * W) at byte
      offset 8 + H + V.
8.7   Emit the attribute blob of length A = sum(Av) at byte
      offset 8 + H + V + I, with each attribute block padded to a
      4-byte boundary per §5.6.
8.8   Emit vertex data as little-endian float32, index data as
      little-endian unsigned integers of width W[k], attribute
      data as little-endian values of the declared type, and
      padding as zero bytes.
8.9   Emit vertex_offset, index_offset, and attr_offset fields
      that form the packed sequences described in sections 6.10,
      6.11, and 6.13.
8.10  Emit a file whose total length equals 8 + H + V + I + A.
8.11  Store vertex positions in world vertex space, applying
      world_matrix as a column vector to each local position
      before writing.
8.12  Store normals, when declared in attr_layout, in world
      vertex space, computed as:

        n_world = normalize( (M[:3,:3])^(-T) · n_local )

      where M[:3,:3] is the 3x3 upper-left block of world_matrix.
      The result MUST be renormalized to unit length after
      transformation.
8.13  If det(M[:3,:3]) < 0, negate n_world after the
      inverse-transpose and before renormalization. This keeps
      normals consistent with winding order for mirrored
      transforms.
8.14  Reject any input whose world_matrix 3x3 block is singular.
8.15  Reject any input primitive whose declared mode is not
      TRIANGLES. If the source primitive has no explicit mode,
      treat it as TRIANGLES.
8.16  Reject any input primitive whose geometry is delivered
      through a compressed intermediate (Draco, meshopt) without
      first decoding it.
8.17  Store indices as ABSOLUTE offsets into the mesh entry's own
      vertex array. MUST NOT store delta-encoded indices.
8.18  Select index_width per §6.12.
8.19  Sort attr_layout by name per §6.15.
8.20  Canonicalize the header JSON per §6.17.
8.21  Emit material_name as JSON null when material_index == -1,
      and as the material's name string or JSON null when
      material_index >= 0.
8.22  Pad each attribute block to a 4-byte boundary within the
      attribute region, per §5.6, and set attr_length to the
      padded sum.

A conforming writer SHOULD:

8.23  Emit uvs and normals attributes for every descriptor where
      the input supplies them, and omit them where the input does
      not.
8.24  Emit descriptors in the order in which meshes are traversed
      in the source glTF scene graph. Note that a single source
      mesh referenced by multiple nodes (instancing) produces
      multiple descriptors with identical (mesh_index,
      primitive_index) but potentially different world_matrix
      and derived vertex data. This is normal and MUST NOT be
      treated as an error by readers.

A conforming writer MUST NOT:

8.25  Emit a file that would fail any MUST-reject rule in
      section 7 when read back.
8.26  Emit non-deterministic output for the same input and the
      same writer version. The "input" here is the source glTF
      document and the writer version; a writer version that
      canonicalizes identically to another version MUST produce
      the same bytes. Extension fields (attr_layout entries with
      "ext:" names) are the responsibility of the extension spec
      to define deterministically; a writer that emits them
      guarantees determinism for the fields it emits, not for
      fields added by future versions.


================================================================
9. FORWARD COMPATIBILITY
================================================================

A conforming reader MUST ignore descriptor fields not listed in
section 6, and MUST NOT reject a descriptor because it contains
such fields. Unknown descriptor fields are forward-compatible.

A conforming reader MUST accept JSON null in fields marked
nullable and MUST reject JSON null in fields marked required.

A conforming reader MUST reject any file whose magic octet
(fourth byte) is not 0x03. Unknown magic octets are NOT
forward-compatible: a version 1.2 reader MUST NOT attempt
best-effort parsing of a file bearing a different magic octet.
Version 1.2 is an incompatible successor to 1.0 and 1.1; those
files MUST be rejected.

A future minor version of this format MAY add new optional
attributes to attr_layout under namespaced extension names, and
MAY add new optional descriptor fields. Any such addition MUST
specify whether the field is emitted deterministically by
writers that emit it.

A future major version MUST use a new magic octet and MAY change
the region layout, field set, or binary types.


================================================================
10. SIZE FORMULA AND SIZE NOTES
================================================================

10.1 Exact formulas

  For a tri-map with K mesh entries:

    V  =  sum over k of (Nv[k] * 12)
    I  =  sum over k of (Nt[k] * 3 * W[k])
    A  =  sum over k of Av[k]

    where Av[k]  =  sum over attributes in attr_layout[k] of
                    padded_align(Nv[k] * components * sizeof(type), 4)

  file_length = 8 + H + V + I + A

10.2 Header size

  The header length H is the byte length of the canonicalized
  JSON array of descriptors. It is bounded below by
  approximately 300 bytes per descriptor for descriptors
  containing world_matrix and fixed fields, plus approximately
  40 bytes per attribute declared in attr_layout. H does not
  grow with Nv; the vertex, index, and attribute blobs are the
  only components that scale with geometry.

10.3 No cross-descriptor sharing

  TMB does not define shared vertex or index arrays between mesh
  entries. Instanced primitives (the same source mesh referenced
  by multiple nodes) appear as independent descriptors, each
  carrying its own copy of the transformed vertices, indices,
  and attributes. This is a size cost; it is required for
  readers to operate on each descriptor independently without
  cross-references. A deduplicating writer MAY detect that two
  descriptors would produce byte-identical vertex, index, and
  attribute data and emit only one, but MUST NOT do so unless
  the source allows it and the writer documents the choice.

10.4 Attributes not included in H

  The header contains only attr_layout descriptions, not
  attribute data. UVs and normals therefore contribute to A,
  not to H. This is the primary reason TMB is smaller than the
  JSON variant on assets with attributes.


================================================================
11. RELATIONSHIP TO JSON TRI-MAP VARIANT
================================================================

The JSON variant (extension .json) is a human-readable,
non-canonical serialization differing from TMB in the following
ways:

  - Indices are delta-encoded (tag "index_encoding": "delta").
    TMB MUST NOT contain delta-encoded indices (§8.17).

  - Vertex coordinates are stored as decimal strings rounded to
    4 decimal places using round-half-to-even (banker's rounding).
    TMB stores IEEE 754 binary32. These are not byte-identical in
    general. Consumers needing bit-exact equivalence use TMB;
    consumers needing human readability use JSON.

  - UVs and normals MAY appear inline as JSON arrays of numbers.
    TMB stores them in the attribute blob as typed binary data.

  - The JSON variant does not require canonicalization and is
    not suitable for content hashing or byte-comparison.

A tool that consumes both variants MUST normalize them to the same
in-memory representation before comparison, treating JSON decimal
strings as approximately equal to TMB binary32 values within the
tolerance in §7.25 (for normals) or within a tolerance of
1e-3 * |value| (for positions).


================================================================
12. SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
================================================================

Sections 7.30 through 7.32 allow a reader to impose
implementation-defined limits on header length, descriptor
count, and total file length. These reader-imposed maxima,
combined with the arithmetic consistency check in §7.21, are
what actually bound resource consumption. Section 7.21 alone
merely ensures that the declared sizes are internally
consistent with the actual file size; it is not by itself a
defense against a well-formed but very large file.

Section 7.22 constrains index values to valid offsets into the
mesh entry's own vertex array, preventing out-of-bounds reads in
readers that do not otherwise bounds-check index access.

Sections 7.23, 7.24, 7.26, and 7.29 exclude signaling NaN,
infinity, and quiet NaN from both vertex and attribute data.
Readers that load data directly into GPU buffers without
validation are otherwise vulnerable to floating-point anomalies
in downstream shading.

Section 8.16 requires decoding of compressed intermediates
before conversion, preventing a writer from silently emitting
unusable geometry for a compressed source.

Sections 8.20 and 8.26 require canonical and deterministic
output, enabling content hashing and signature verification of
TMB files.


